PMID 38698650 YEAR 2024 NCT NCT03548935 DOI 10.1111/dom.15620 PUBTYPES ['Journal Article', 'Clinical Trial, Phase III', 'Randomized Controlled Trial', "Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't"]
TITLE: Effect of semaglutide 2.4 mg on physical functioning and weight- and health-related quality of life in adults with overweight or obesity: Patient-reported outcomes from the STEP 1-4 trials.
RULE: INCLUDE eligible double-blind/placebo-controlled RCT: intervention semaglutide 2.4, comparator placebo, population overweight — P/I/C/design met. SPAN: population “…of life in adults with overweight or obesity: Patient-rep…”; comparator “…maglutide 2.4 mg versus placebo in individuals with ove…”
AIMS: To summarize the effects of semaglutide 2.4 mg on weight-related quality of life (WRQOL) and health-
related quality of life (HRQOL), focusing on the confirmatory secondary endpoint of physical functioning.
MATERIALS AND METHODS: The STEP 1-4 Phase 3a, 68-week, double-blind, randomized controlled trials assessed the
efficacy and safety of semaglutide 2.4 mg versus placebo in individuals with overweight/obesity. WRQOL and
HRQOL were assessed by change from baseline to Week 68 in two different but complementary measures, the Impact
of Weight on Quality of Life-Lite Clinical Trials Version (IWQOL-Lite-CT; STEP 1 and 2) and the SF-36v2 Health
Survey Acute (SF-36v2; STEP 1-4). RESULTS: Superiority for semaglutide 2.4 mg over placebo based on IWQOL-
Lite-CT and SF-36v2 physical functioning scores was confirmed in STEP 1 and 2 and in STEP 1, 2 and 4,
respectively. At Week 68, a greater proportion of participants treated with semaglutide 2.4 mg than with
placebo reached meaningful within-person change (MWPC) thresholds for IWQOL-Lite-CT Physical Function scores
in STEP 1 (51.8% vs. 28.3%; p < 0.0001) and STEP 2 (39.6% vs. 29.5%; p = 0.0083) and the MWPC threshold for
SF-36v2 Physical Functioning in STEP 1 (39.8% vs. 24.1%; p < 0.0001), STEP 2 (41.0% vs. 27.3%; p = 0.0005) and
STEP 4 (18.0% vs. 6.6%; p < 0.0001). All other IWQOL-Lite-CT and SF-36v2 scale scores in STEP 1-4 were
numerically improved with semaglutide 2.4 mg versus placebo, except for SF-36v2 Role Emotional in STEP 2.
CONCLUSIONS: Semaglutide 2.4 mg significantly improved physical functioning, with greater proportions of
participants achieving MWPC compared with placebo, and showed beneficial effects on WRQOL and HRQOL beyond
physical functioning.

